The NISDCC signature collection was generated in cooperation by:

    Dr. Louis Vuurpijl (l.vuurpijl@donders.ru.nl)
    Donders Institute for Brain, Cognition and Behaviour
    Centre for Cognition (acronym: "DCC")
    Radboud University Nijmegen

and

    Dr. Katrin Franke (kyfranke@ieee.org)
    Norwegian Information Security Laboratory (NISlab, acronym: "NIS"),
    Department of Computer Science and Media Technology,
    Gjovik University College

The name "NISDCC" is derived from the acronyms from both the NISlab (NIS) and
Donders Centre for Cognition (DCC). These data are made publicly available for:

  (i)  the ICDAR'09 signature verification competition and
  (ii) research purposes

Any use of these data should mention reference [1]:

[1] C.E. van den Heuvel, K. Franke, L. Vuurpijl (et al),
    The ICDAR 2009 signature verification competition,
    In ICDAR2009 proceedings (will be updated)


Dataset Information:
====================
The NISDCC signature collection was acquired in the framework of the WANDA
project [2], a cooperative effort by Katrin Franke, Lambert Schomaker, and
Louis Vuurpijl. The collection contains simultaneously acquired online and
offline samples. The online dataset is called "NISDCC-online" and was processed
and segmented by Louis Vuurpijl. The offline dataset is called "NISDCC-offline"
and was scanned by Linda Alewijnse and Elisa van den Heuvel from the NFI. The
offline signatures were automatically segmented and visually inspected by Louis
Vuurpijl. Processing of the segmented signatures was performed by Katrin
Franke. The collection contains:

* authentic signatures from 12 writers (each writer wrote his signature 5 times)
* forged signatures from 31 writers (5 forgeries per authentic signature)

This README concerns the online samples. 
     _____________________  
    | +                 + |  Each writer was asked to write each signature 5
    |   _____             |  times on an A4 sheet of paper. Writers wrote with
    |  |__1__|            |  an inking digitizer pen on a Wacom A4-oversized
    |                     |  tablet sampled at 200Hz at 100dpi resolution. The
    |             _____   |  sheet of paper was fixed to the tablet on top of
    |            |__2__|  |  a boxed form containing 5 boxes, organized as
    |                     |  depicted to the left.
    |   _____             |  Most writers wrote inside or across these boxes,
    |  |__3__|            |  which were visible through the (blank) A4 paper.
    |                     |
    |             _____   |  This layout enabled relatively straight-forward
    |            |__4__|  |  segmentation in both the offline and online
    |                     |  domains. For calibration between the acquired
    |   _____             |  online pen-tip trajectories and scanned offline
    |  |__5__|            |  documents, writers were asked to mark four crosses
    |                     |  located in the corners of the A4 paper.
    |_+_________________+_|

Online recording conditions and data collection details
=======================================================
The 43 writers were students in Psychology and Artificial Intelligence and
employees of the DCC (former NICI). Among these writers were five experts in
handwriting recognition and signature verification. Writers were instructed to
(i) practice in writing authentic or copying forged signatures as much as they
found necessary, using the Intuos inking pen on an A4 paper sheet on top of the
tablet, (ii) after practicing, mark the four calibration crosses as depicted
above, and (iii) write each signature in the given order in the corresponding
boxes. Forgers were requested to copy signatures as fluently as possible, while
trying to mimic the shape of the authentic signature as much as possible. Due
to the difference in forgery skills, the quality of forgeries in the NISDCC
collection is quite variable.

During all recordings, an experimenter was present, guiding the writers in the
data acquisition process. The task of the experimenter was to collect writer
details, fix each A4 sheet of paper properly, and start recording the online
samples by pressing a button. Writer details are not provided in the current
distribution (version 1.0). All but one writer wrote in or near the required
boxes. This writer <fff>=031 wrote 8 pages (5 signatures each) starting at the
upper-right location (in stead of upper-left). All but one writer wrote in the
correct vertical order. During writing of the third signature of <aaa>=12
(middle row), writer <fff>=044 experienced a dried up inking pen. This
signature (labeled NISDCC-044_012_03.hwr) was re-written at the proper vertical
box orientation, but located on the right-side of the page.

The NISDCC-online signature collection was recorded using:
	- Wacom Intuos2 A4-oversized tablet
	- Wacom Intuos inking pen (XP-110-MOD)
	- Pentium-III Intell PC operating at 400MHz, 64MB Ram
	- Windows98, 1st Edition (Release 4.10, 1998)
	- Wacom tablet driver Version: 4.75-9
    - "SIC Tablet" tablet-interface developed by the Frauenhofer IPK, Berlin

Writer identifications and filenames
====================================
Each writer is identified via a three-digit identifier. The 12 authentic
writers are identified as <aaa>={001,002,...012}. The 31 forgers are identified
as <fff>={021,022,...,051}. Each signature is contained in a separate file,
"NISDCC-<www>_<aaa>_<iii>" with extension ".hwr" (for description of the hwr
data format, see below). Note that the writer identification <www> can refer to
an authentic writer or forger. The index <idx> represents the position of the
signature on the A4 sheet of paper. So, the file NISDCC-001_001_001.hwr contains
the first signature written by the authentic writer <aaa>=001. Similarly, the
file NISDCC-051_012_005.hwr contains the last forged signature from <aaa>=012
written by forger <fff>=051.

Online "hwr" Data Format:
=========================
The hwr (from HandWriting Recording) format is straight-forward. Each file
contains a header line:

	<label> <npoints>

The <label> is similar to the filename, without the ".hwr" extension. The
<npoints> denotes the number of subsequent sampled trajectory points. Each
point contains five recorded pen-tip coordinates (x,y,p,a,e). For a detailed
discussion on these pen coordinates (or channels), please consider [3]. Below,
each channel is briefly described. Note that recording conditions were kept
constant for all cases, which makes cross-signature comparisons feasible.

	x = x-position on tablet (raw tablet values)
	y = y-position on tablet (raw tablet values), (0,0) is bottom-left (!)
	p = pen pressure in raw tablet values, between [0-1023]
	a = azimuth angle of the pen (yaw) between [0-3600] (10*degrees)
	e = elevation angle of the pen (pitch) between [0-900] (10*degrees)

In occasions where the writer lifted the pen too far from the tablet to be
sampled, the pressure coordinate encodes a PENFAR value and all other
coordinates are 0. The PENFAR value is -4.

Size of the NISDCC-online signature collection
=============================================
The NISDCC-online signature collection contains in total 1905 signature files.
Three pages containing five signatures each are missing due to an error during
recording (the experimenter forgot to press the OK-button which triggered the
recording of tablet data). The experimenter was punished severely for these
errors. The missing pages are: <www>_<aaa> = { 021_006, 028_005, 046_011 }.
Since these 15 forged signatures are missing, the total number of forgeries is
1845 (31*12*5-15) The total number of authentic signatures is 12x5 = 60. So the
total number of signatures contained in the NISDCC-online collection is 1905.

